{{- /*
The delegating override: correct everywhere except one statement.

A working reference behind the double, one method replaced by a closure
that alters what it is handed and forwards the rest. Every claim of the
form "does the right thing except for X" needs this shape, and a bare
stub cannot express it — a double with no delegate stores nothing, so a
check about what survives or expires has nothing to observe and the red
says nothing about the claim.

The delegate is built ONCE and closed over. Calling the constructor
again inside the override would forward to a second, empty instance:
every write would land somewhere the reads never look, which is a
different defect and a much less interesting one.
*/ -}}
{{- define "suite.defect.delegated-override" -}}
{{ renderExpr (external $.Prove "One") }}({{ printf "%q" .DefectName }},
	func(tb {{ renderExpr (external "testing" "TB") }}) {{ renderExpr (external .Pkg .Subject) }} {
		inner := {{ renderExpr (external .Pkg .Ref) }}()
		return {{ renderExpr (external .Pkg .Ctor) }}(tb,
			{{ renderExpr (external .Pkg .DelegateTo) }}(inner),
			{{ renderExpr (external .Pkg .Option) }}(
				func{{ renderParams .ForwardParams }} {{ renderReturns .AnonReturns }} {
					{{ .Mutate }}
					return inner.{{ .Method }}({{ .ForwardArgs }})
				}))
	}){{ template "suite.frag.defect-reason" . }}
{{- end -}}
